window.docContents[209].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Programming-with-Tactics":{"contents":"\n\nIf we can build proofs by giving explicit terms rather than\nexecuting tactic scripts, you may wonder whether we can build\nprograms using tactics rather than by writing down explicit\nterms.Naturally, the answer is yes!def add2 : Nat → Nat := by\n  show_proof\n    intro n\n    apply Nat.succ\n    apply Nat.succ\n    exact n\n?_\n\nafter intro n:\n  fun n => ?_\n\nafter apply Nat.succ:\n  fun n => Nat.succ ?_\n\nafter apply Nat.succ:\n  fun n => Nat.succ (Nat.succ ?_)\n\nafter exact n:\n  fun n => Nat.succ (Nat.succ n)\n#print add2\ndef EvenProp.add2 : Nat → Nat :=\nfun n => n.succ.succ\n#eval add2 2\n4\nNotice that we followed def … := with by rather than\nwith a term.  This tells Lean to enter proof\nscripting mode to build an object of type Nat → Nat.This feature is mainly useful for writing functions with dependent\ntypes, which we won't explore much further in this book.  But it\ndoes illustrate the uniformity and orthogonality of the basic\nideas in Lean.Since def and theorem can both use terms or by, is there any difference between the two?\nThere is one: a theorem must have a Prop type.theorem add2_with_theorem_wrong : Nat → Nat := by\n    apply Nat.succ\ntype of theorem `EvenProp.add2_with_theorem_wrong` is not a proposition\n  Nat → Nat\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.3. Programming with Tactics","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Programming-with-Tactics"}});